  <proposition xml:id="prop123">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>p</m> be a prime and let all matrices be over <m>\mathbb{F}_p</m>. 
            Suppose <m>\bar{y}=e_{1p}</m> and <m>\bar{x}</m> are such that
            <me>\bar{x}\bar{y}=0=\bar{y}\bar{x}.</me>
            If there exist <m>A, B \in M_p(\mathbb{F}_p)</m> such that
            <me>[\bar{x}, B] + [A, \bar{y}] = I_p,</me>
            with the conditions
            <me>B e_p = 0, \qquad \operatorname{Im}(x_c b_r)\subset \mathbb{F}_pe_1 \text{ (this is equivalent to } b_r=0 \text{ or } x_c\in\mathbb{F}_p e_1\text{)}.</me>
            where
            <ul>
                <li><m>b_r</m> is the last row of <m>B</m> (restricted to its first <m>p-1</m> entries),</li>
                <li><m>x_c</m> is the last column of <m>\bar{x}</m> (restricted to its first <m>p-1</m> entries),</li>
                <li><m>x_m</m> is the top-left <m>(p-1)\times(p-1)</m> submatrix of <m>\bar{x}</m>,</li>
                <li><m>b_m</m> is the top-left <m>(p-1)\times(p-1)</m> submatrix of <m>B</m>,</li>
            </ul>
            then <m>(x_m, b_m)</m> is a Calogero-Moser pair.
        </p>
        <p>
            Conversely, if <m>(x_m, b_m)</m> is a Calogero-Moser pair such that
            <me>[x_m, b_m] = I_{p-1} + R</me>
            with <m>\operatorname{Im}(R) \subseteq \mathbb{F}_p e_1</m> and <m>x_m e_1 = 0</m>, then for any
            <m>b_r \in \mathbb{F}_p^{\,1\times(p-1)}</m> and <m>x_c \in \mathbb{F}_p^{(p-1)\times 1}</m>
            satisfying
            <me>\operatorname{Im}(x_c b_r)\subset \mathbb{F}_pe_1,</me>
            there exists <m>A \in M_p(\mathbb{F}_p)</m> such that
            <me>[\bar{x}, B] + [A, \bar{y}] = I_p.</me>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
        <p>Sketch of the proof:</p>
        
        <p>
            <term>Block notation.</term>
            Write
            <me>
                \bar{x} = 
                \begin{pmatrix}
                x_m &amp; x_c \\
                0 &amp; 0
                \end{pmatrix}, \qquad
                B = 
                \begin{pmatrix}
                b_m &amp; b \\
                b_r &amp; 0
                \end{pmatrix}.
            </me>
        </p>
        
        <p>
            Set
            <me>
                X_m = 
                \begin{pmatrix}
                x_m &amp; 0 \\
                0 &amp; 0
                \end{pmatrix}, \qquad
                B_m = 
                \begin{pmatrix}
                b_m &amp; 0 \\
                0 &amp; 0
                \end{pmatrix},
            </me>
            <me>
                X_c = 
                \begin{pmatrix}
                0 &amp; x_c \\
                0 &amp; 0
                \end{pmatrix}, \qquad
                B_r = 
                \begin{pmatrix}
                0 &amp; 0 \\
                b_r &amp; 0
                \end{pmatrix}.
            </me>
        </p>
        
        <p>
            Then we have the following commutators:
            <me>
                [B_r, X_m] = 
                \begin{pmatrix}
                0 &amp; 0 \\
                b_r x_m &amp; 0
                \end{pmatrix},
            </me>
            <me>
                [B_m, X_c] = 
                \begin{pmatrix}
                0 &amp; b_m x_c \\
                0 &amp; 0
                \end{pmatrix}, \qquad
                [B_r, X_c] = 
                \begin{pmatrix}
                -x_c b_r &amp; 0 \\
                0 &amp; b_r x_c
                \end{pmatrix}.
            </me>
        </p>
        
        <p>
            In addition
            <me>
                [A,\bar{y}] = 
                \begin{pmatrix}
                -a_{p1} &amp; -a_{p2} &amp; \cdots &amp; a_{11}-a_{pp} \\
                0 &amp; \cdots &amp; \cdots &amp; a_{21} \\
                \vdots &amp; \ddots &amp; \ddots &amp; \vdots \\
                0 &amp; 0 &amp; \cdots &amp; a_{p1}
                \end{pmatrix},
            </me>
            with
            <me>
                x_c = 
                \begin{pmatrix}
                x_{1p} \\
                x_{2p} \\
                \vdots \\
                x_{p-1,p}
                \end{pmatrix}, \qquad
                b_r = 
                \begin{pmatrix}
                b_{p1} &amp; \cdots &amp; b_{p,p-1}
                \end{pmatrix}.
            </me>
            Moreover,
            <me>
                x_c b_r = \bigl( x_{ip}\, b_{pj} \bigr)_{i,j=1}^{p-1}, \qquad
                b_r x_c = \sum_{i=1}^{p-1} b_{pi}\, x_{ip}.
            </me>
        </p>
        
        <p>
            Now, since <m>\bar{x} = X_m + X_c</m> and <m>B = B_m + B_r</m>, we have
            <me>
                [\bar{x}, B] = [X_m + X_c, B_m + B_r]
                = [X_m, B_m] + [X_m, B_r] + [X_c, B_m] + [X_c, B_r].
            </me>
        </p>
        
        <p>
            Using the explicit block commutators, the equation
            <me>[\bar{x}, B] + [A, \bar{y}] = I_p</me>
            is equivalent to
            <me>
                [X_m, B_m]
                =
                I_p - [A,\bar{y}]
                +
                \begin{pmatrix}
                0 &amp; 0 \\
                b_r x_m &amp; 0
                \end{pmatrix}
                +
                \begin{pmatrix}
                0 &amp; b_m x_c \\
                0 &amp; 0
                \end{pmatrix}
                +
                \begin{pmatrix}
                -x_c b_r &amp; 0 \\
                0 &amp; b_r x_c
                \end{pmatrix}.
            </me>
        </p>
        
        <p>
            Taking the top-left <m>(p-1)\times(p-1)</m> block gives
            <me>
                [x_m, b_m]
                =
                I_{p-1}
                +
                \begin{pmatrix}
                a_{p1} &amp; a_{p2} &amp; \cdots &amp; a_{p,p-1} \\
                0 &amp; 0 &amp; \cdots &amp; 0 \\
                \vdots &amp; \vdots &amp; \ddots &amp; \vdots \\
                0 &amp; 0 &amp; \cdots &amp; 0
                \end{pmatrix}
                -
                x_c b_r.
            </me>
        </p>
        
        <p>
            This yields the Calogero-Moser condition for <m>(x_m,b_m)</m>, precisely when <m>\operatorname{Im}(x_c b_r)\subset \mathbb{F}_pe_1</m>.
        </p>
    </proof>
</proposition>



</worksheet>
